Fix for clingox UPSTREAM-ISSUES U19: clasp's registry of statistic types is
not thread-safe.

StatisticObject::registerType appends to the global vector types_s without a
lock. Types register lazily, from function-local statics, one per template
instance, so two threads that first use two different kinds of statistics
append at once, and tid() on a third thread reads the vector while it may be
reallocating: a data race and a possible use-after-free. With this patch the
registry is a fixed array of 1024 entries (clasp has a few dozen types; ids
are 16 bits), registerType takes a mutex in threaded builds and aborts with a
message on overflow, and tid() reads a slot without a lock. That is sound
because a reader only holds ids returned after their slot was written, and the
thread-safe initialization of the statics that hold them orders the write
before the read.

One might ask whether the `#if CLASP_HAS_THREADS` guard around the
mutex could be dropped, since clingo 5.8 requires C++11 where it is used.
Checked against clingox-sys/build.rs and clasp/CMakeLists.txt: with the
`threads` feature off, CLASP_BUILD_WITH_THREADS is OFF, and clasp's own
CMakeLists.txt then sets CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD to 98 for clasp's own targets,
which clingox-sys's build does not override. Verified on this host: a
threads-off build of clingox-sys compiles clasp/src/statistics.cpp with
-std=gnu++98, and <mutex> does not compile under that standard with this
toolchain. The guard therefore stays; registerType keeps the pre-patch race in
that one configuration, which UPSTREAM-ISSUES U19 and the guide's known issues
now say explicitly. Against clingo v5.8.2; suitable for upstream.

